{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The claim asserts resumption is 'always safe at any point of exhaustion' under idempotency, but its own countercondition identifies a genuine fault: a token-budget exhaustion can leave a partial event in the append-only log that 'lacks a complete state representation.' The mechanism provides no causal pathway for how resumption handles this partial event — it merely asserts 'resumption simply appends new identical events or continues deterministically.' If the exhausted run left a half-written event record, resuming by appending after it (or ignoring it) either leaves a corrupt intermediate record or silently drops it, both of which can corrupt append-only identity. The problem specifically asks about token-budget exhaustion corrupting append-only identity, and SRC_001's mechanism does not address the partial-event artifact that is the central risk.","target":"003d42d6905bee46fa5c5cbff036062647e71d1045b81f660aa6f316e6167f00"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 sidesteps the problem entirely. The problem asks 'when it may later be resumed without corrupting the append-only identity of the recorded events, and when resumption must stay refused.' Treating resumption as 'a new derived branch rather than a continuation' is not resumption at all — it is launching a separate run. The causal mechanism offered is only that 'the original stream is immutable,' which is trivially true of any append-only log and says nothing about whether the branch preserves causal continuity with the exhausted run's intermediate state. Furthermore, by declaring resumption 'always permissible,' SRC_002 provides no refusal criterion, failing to 'adjudicate between rival criteria' or determine when resumption must stay refused as the problem demands.","target":"6436c6b2d5ed6545efc2cfc068177e20a89feaea14c957727ad283175dd28896"},{"attack":true,"case":"The causal fingerprint (program_hash, input_hash, model_checkpoint_hash) is computed at the run start, but the problem concerns resuming from the exhaustion point, which is mid-stream. The fingerprint verifies that the environment at resumption matches the environment at start, but this does not causally guarantee that the intermediate execution state at the exhaustion point is valid or reconstructable. Token-budget exhaustion can occur at different points across attempts due to factors not captured by the fingerprint (e.g., non-deterministic token counting, internal scheduler state). The mechanism conflates start-state determinism with mid-stream resumption safety: matching start fingerprints ensures that a fresh re-run would be deterministic, but the claim is about resuming from the partial state, which the fingerprint does not verify. The biconditional 'if and only if' is therefore too strong — fingerprint match is neither necessary (resumption from a verified checkpoint could be safe even if the model was updated, if the exhausted portion didn't depend on it) nor sufficient (the intermediate state at the exhaustion point may be corrupt or unreconstructable despite matching start fingerprints).","target":"90e14e60b044214019e48bc1a4b14643578858782fe991ee10df47b45081431f"},{"attack":true,"case":"The claim that resumption must 'always be refused' is internally contradicted by its own countercondition, which concedes that in deterministic, stateless systems with deterministic replay, resumption is 'logically indistinguishable from re-execution.' The problem asks to 'determine exactly when it may later be resumed' — SRC_004 refuses this determination, offering only a blanket refusal that its own countercondition undermines. From a causal-mechanism standpoint, the mechanism asserts 'any attempt to resume risks subtle state corruption that cannot be proven safe' but identifies no specific causal pathway by which append-only identity would be corrupted. Without specifying what state corruption occurs and how it propagates to the event log, the blanket refusal is an assertion of caution rather than a causal explanation, and it fails to adjudicate between rival criteria as the problem requires.","target":"a1ea46aa1f2299559cb5c2daaf69316a2a8ffa6c344a7d82673b04e85a96641b"}]}